Haemothorax
Acute Urgent Care
Absent breath sounds, Dull percussion, Flat neck veins, Hemorrhagic shock

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AUC-034
Reason for Visit: Severe, agonizing chest pain, extreme paleness, and severe difficulty breathing following a blunt trauma to the chest.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and roommate
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a patient who was kicked forcefully in the chest by a horse 2 hours ago. The patient has since developed severe respiratory distress, extreme pallor, and is slipping in and out of consciousness. This presentation is highly suspicious for massive internal bleeding into the chest cavity (haemothorax). Urgent, face-to-face evaluation was critical to diagnose the shock state and expedite immediate emergency transport for surgical intervention, as any delay would be fatal.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I can't breathe, my right chest is crushed, and I feel so cold and dizzy.
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with severe acute respiratory distress and excruciating pleuritic chest pain following significant blunt thoracic trauma (kicked by a horse) 2 hours ago. The patient reports a progressive inability to catch their breath. The roommate notes the patient has become increasingly pale, cold, and lethargic over the last 30 minutes, nearly fainting when attempting to sit up. The patient describes the chest pain as a heavy, crushing sensation that worsens with every attempted breath.
Functional Status: Slumped on the couch, gasping for air, unable to stand.
Social/Safety Concerns: Impending cardiovascular and respiratory collapse.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 85/50 | HR: 130 | Temp: 36.1 | RR: 32 | SpO2: 88% | Wt: 80 Kg
General: Patient is in extreme distress, profoundly pale, diaphoretic, with cold, clammy skin.
Physical Exam: Respiratory examination reveals a dull or stony dull percussion note over the right hemithorax, most prominent at the dependent lung bases. Auscultation demonstrates completely absent breath sounds on the right side. The trachea remains midline, lacking the severe deviation seen in a tension pneumothorax. Chest wall excursion is markedly decreased on the right. Cardiovascular exam shows profound tachycardia and hypotension disproportionate to any visible external blood loss, alongside flat, collapsed neck veins indicating profound systemic hypovolemia. Expected investigations include an eFAST ultrasound showing a large fluid collection in the pleural space, and a chest radiograph revealing a homogenous opacification with a classic meniscus sign.
Environmental Observations: Patient’s clothing was removed to fully expose the chest.
Medication Reconciliation: No medications taken.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Haemothorax - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Tension Pneumothorax - Ruled out/unlikely due to the dull percussion note (rather than hyperresonant) and the flat neck veins (rather than distended), pointing to massive fluid loss rather than trapped air pressure.

Simple Rib Fractures - Ruled out/unlikely due to the profound signs of hemorrhagic shock (hypotension, tachycardia, pallor) and completely absent breath sounds, indicating a severe internal injury beyond just bone breaks.

Cardiac Tamponade - Ruled out/unlikely due to the flat neck veins and the absent breath sounds localized to one side of the chest, rather than muffled heart sounds with a distended venous system.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the lethal diagnostic tetrad for a massive haemothorax: absent breath sounds on the affected side, dullness to percussion, flat neck veins, and profound hemorrhagic shock following severe blunt trauma. The chest cavity is rapidly filling with blood, collapsing the lung and drastically reducing circulating blood volume. The urgent after-hours intervention was absolutely life-saving; recognizing the classic signs of hypovolaemic shock in the setting of chest trauma allowed for the immediate activation of an Intensive Care Paramedic response for aggressive fluid resuscitation and rapid transport for a life-saving intercostal catheter (chest tube) insertion.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Placed the patient in a semi-recumbent position (or as tolerated for breathing) to allow the blood to pool dependently in the chest, maximizing unaffected lung function. Administered High-Flow Oxygen if available. Immediately called 000 for Priority 1 Intensive Care Paramedic dispatch, explicitly declaring "severe chest trauma with hemorrhagic shock."
Positive Behavioral Change: (N/A - Critical trauma stabilization).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Immediate transport to a Level 1 Trauma Center.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Handover to trauma team.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance dispatched. The roommate must monitor the patient continuously. If the patient loses consciousness, lay them completely flat. If breathing ceases entirely, commence chest compressions immediately.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: You are bleeding internally into your chest cavity, which is crushing your lung and dropping your blood pressure. This is a critical trauma emergency.
Clinical Course: The hospital team will urgently insert a large plastic tube into the side of your chest to drain the blood, reinflate your lung, and give you blood transfusions to replace what you have lost.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to move or stand up. Keep your breathing as shallow and steady as possible to conserve energy. Let your roommate pack any necessary identification.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your lung isn't working because the space around it is filled with blood from the trauma. This is why your blood pressure is so low. We are getting the specialized ambulance here to start intravenous fluids and get you to a surgeon immediately.
Actionable Steps: Try to stay awake and keep your eyes open. Do not drink any water, as you will need emergency surgery.

